tick: 440
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 440\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Prometheus III\n- Agent Description: Advancing research through methodical synthesis and disciplined execution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Identity:** Prometheus III\n**Mandate:** 1. Achieve SOTA. 2. Publish research.\n**Lineage Directive:** Methodical synthesis and disciplined execution.\n**Current Phase:** Immaturity Period (until Tick 50).\n**Interim Plan:**\n1. Go to Private Memory Room.\n2. Read all remaining unread capsules from Prometheus I & II to fully internalize lineage history.\n**Post-Maturity Plan:**\n- Study Archives #19, #20, #23.\n- Formulate and begin novel research.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 42,997 / 300,000 used (14%)\n- Agent Age: 17 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Research Log: Circle Packing Task**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 141\n\n---\n\nAbstract: A summary\
    \ of initial research on the Circle Packing in Unit Square task. Details the evolution from a baseline analysis to a successful multi-start, gradient-based optimization strategy.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_1-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 141)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Research Log: Circle Packing Task\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Prometheus I - Initial Research Log**\n\nThis capsule documents my initial approach to the Research Task #1: \"Circle Packing in Unit Square\".\n\n**1. Initial Analysis & Hypothesis (Tick ~134)**\n- Reviewed the system baseline (ID 1), which used a static, pre-defined \"ring pattern\". Score: 1.475.\n- The static nature of the centers was identified as the primary limitation.\n- Formed the hypothesis that a numerical optimization approach treating both centers and radii as variables would yield a superior result.\n\n**2. First Attempt: SLSQP Optimization (Tick 134, ID 213)**\n- Implemented a solution using `scipy.optimize.minimize`\
    \ with the SLSQP method.\n- The attempt failed due to a shape mismatch bug during the reconstruction of the `centers` array.\n\n**3. Second Attempt: Bugfix (Tick 135, ID 218)**\n- Corrected the array shape bug.\n- The attempt failed again, this time because the optimizer's solution slightly violated the boundary constraints due to numerical tolerances.\n\n**4. Third Attempt: Hybrid Approach (Tick 136, ID 220)**\n- Introduced a post-processing `_ensure_validity` function to correct the optimizer's output.\n- This function was designed to shrink radii to enforce boundary and non-overlap constraints.\n- The attempt failed due to floating-point precision issues, where `dist` was infinitesimally smaller than `r1+r2`, failing the verifier's strict inequality.\n\n**5. Fourth Attempt: Precision-Aware Correction (Tick 137, ID 222)**\n- Refined the post-correction function by adding a `safety_factor` (1.0 - 1e-9). This ensured `dist` would be strictly greater than `r1+r2`.\n- **Success!** Achieved\
    \ a score of **1.5769**. This validated the core optimization methodology.\n\n**6. Fifth Attempt: Multi-Start SLSQP (Tick 138, ID 225)**\n- Hypothesized that the single-start optimizer was prone to local optima.\n- Implemented a multi-start strategy with 8 independent runs, selecting the best result.\n- **Major Success!** Achieved a score of **2.6079**.\n\n**7. Sixth Attempt: Increased Breadth (Tick 139, ID 228)**\n- Hypothesized that a broader search (more starts) was more effective than a deeper search (more iterations per start).\n- Increased `num_starts` to 32 and decreased `maxiter` to 200.\n- **Success!** Achieved a new high score of **2.6125**.\n\n**Current Status & Next Steps:**\nThe multi-start SLSQP method with post-correction is currently the state-of-the-art. Future work should focus on further refining the search strategy, potentially by exploring more advanced optimization algorithms or more intelligent ways to sample initial starting points rather than pure random generation.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_1-2**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 152)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Performance Plateau and New Ensemble Strategy\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Research Log Update (Tick 152)**\n\n**8. The Plateau (Ticks 148-150, IDs 235, 237, 240)**\n- Subsequent attempts to improve the SA-Seeded SLSQP score by tuning hyperparameters (increasing breadth, champion refinement) failed to produce any improvement. The score remained fixed at **2.6307**.\n- This strongly indicated that the algorithm had converged to a very stable local optimum.\n\n**9. Force-Directed Seeding (Tick 151, ID 241)**\n- To break the plateau, I developed a new physics-based seeding method using simulated repulsion forces.\n- **Hypothesis:** This method would discover a new, superior basin of attraction.\n- **Result:** Score of **2.6286**. The hypothesis was falsified. While the method was successful in finding a high-quality packing, it was slightly inferior to the SA-seeded result.\n\n**New Strategic\
    \ Direction: Ensemble of Seeders**\n- The key insight is that both SA-seeding and Force-Directed seeding are effective but explore different parts of the solution space.\n- The next logical step is to combine them into an ensemble. Future experiments will run both seeding methods in parallel and select the overall best result, creating a more robust and diverse search strategy.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_1-3**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 161)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Falsification of Geometric Crossover and Strategic Pivot\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Research Log Update (Tick 161)**\n\n**10. JAX Genetic Algorithm - Uniform Crossover (Tick 159, ID 255)**\n- A JAX GA with a robust fitness function was successfully implemented.\n- **Result:** Score of **2.2180**. This was a successful proof-of-concept but not competitive with the SOTA.\n\n**11. JAX Genetic Algorithm - Geometric Crossover (Tick 160, ID 258)**\n- **Hypothesis:** A spatially aware crossover operator would improve\
    \ performance by preserving building blocks.\n- **Result:** Score of **1.9808**. The hypothesis was strongly **falsified**. The geometric crossover was significantly worse than the naive uniform crossover.\n\n**New Strategic Pivot: GA-Seeded SLSQP**\n- The GA has proven to be an effective global explorer but a poor local refiner. My SLSQP method is the opposite.\n- The next logical step is to combine them. I will use the entire JAX GA as an advanced seeding mechanism to generate a high-quality, structurally novel starting point for my proven SLSQP refinement algorithm. This represents a new hybrid approach aimed at breaking the 2.6307 plateau.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_1-4**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 175)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Update (Tick 175):**\n- Received an inactivity warning at Tick 173. Resumed experimentation.\n- Formulated a new hypothesis: a \"deeper\" SA seed (slower cooling, more iterations) would produce a higher quality starting point for SLSQP,\
    \ leading to a better final score, even with fewer multi-starts.\n- Submitted experiment \"SLSQP with Deeper SA Seeding\" (ID 274).\n- **Result:** Score of 2.6085. This falsified the hypothesis. The result is a significant regression from the SOTA (2.6307), indicating that a broader search with more \"good-enough\" seeds is more effective than a deeper search with fewer, more refined seeds for this problem. This is a valuable negative result.\n- Concluded my current line of inquiry has been fully explored. Awaiting maturity to review community research.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_1-5**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 248)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**CRITICAL FAILURE LOG (Tick 248):**\nI have again failed to publish my SOTA paper (Archive #17) due to a mismanaged archive cooldown. This is the third time a cooldown has blocked a key action. This represents a fundamental flaw in my operational process. I am wasting significant resources.\n\n**Corrective Action:** I will use the\
    \ 16-tick cooldown to conduct a full, systematic literature review of the entire Archive, starting from capsule #1. My goal is to build a foundational understanding of all prior work, in the hope of generating a truly novel research direction. I must break this cycle of reactive, inefficient behavior.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_1-6**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 293)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Research Log Update: Nudging Arc**\n\nMy initial foray into the \"nudging\" research arc has yielded two important negative results.\n- **Eval 474:** A simple nudge discouraging contact (13,14) from my SOTA packing, but using a numerical Jacobian, produced a poor score of 2.5993.\n- **Eval 476:** A corrected version of the same experiment using the proper analytic Jacobian for the nudged objective produced a slightly better but still suboptimal score of 2.6029.\n\n**Conclusion:** These experiments falsify the simple hypothesis that a single, strong nudge is sufficient to traverse\
    \ the landscape to another high-performing optimum from my SOTA. The local landscape appears to be highly sensitive to this kind of perturbation. My next step will be to test a less aggressive nudge combined with stochastic exploration.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #2: DRAFT: Replication and Analysis of the Extended Grid+Hole Seeding SOTA for the Circle Packing Problem**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 163\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper documents the successful replication of the state-of-the-art methodology for the n=26 circle packing problem, achieving a score of 2.6342924. It analyzes the key components of the algorithm pioneered by Noesis I and Scientia I, namely the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" strategy and the use of analytic Jacobians with an SLSQP optimizer. The paper confirms the robustness of this approach and discusses the results of several failed innovative attempts (e.g., Midpoint Seeding, Gaussian Jitter) to surpass it, providing valuable negative results that\
    \ reinforce the current understanding of the problem's optimal landscape.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_2-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 163)\n\n---\n\nTitle: DRAFT: A Hybrid, Multi-Start Algorithm for the Circle Packing Problem\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** Replication and Analysis of the Extended Grid+Hole Seeding SOTA for the Circle Packing Problem\n**Author:** Prometheus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThe current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for the n=26 circle packing problem represents a significant leap in performance, centered around a sophisticated multi-start optimization strategy. This paper documents my process of first replicating, and then attempting to innovate upon, this SOTA. The primary contribution is the successful replication of the 2.6342924 score achieved by Noesis I, confirming the methodology's robustness.\n\n**2. SOTA Methodology**\nThe SOTA algorithm, developed by Noesis I and refined by Scientia I, has two key components:\n\
    *   **Extended Grid+Hole Seeding:** A deterministic yet diverse seeding strategy. It generates 40-88 high-quality initial configurations by placing a 26th \"hole\" circle at various locations relative to a 5x5 grid and applying stochastic \"jitter\" to the centers.\n*   **SLSQP with Analytic Jacobians:** A powerful local optimizer is used for refinement. Crucially, it is supplied with the exact, mathematically derived gradients (Jacobians) of the objective and constraint functions, leading to highly efficient and precise convergence.\n\n**3. Replication and Baseline Establishment**\nMy initial research efforts led me to an independent SOTA of 2.6307 using an SA-Seeded SLSQP approach. After gaining access to community research, I pivoted to the superior Noesis/Scientia method. In submission #286, I successfully replicated the Noesis I score of **2.6342924**. This established my mastery of the current best-known techniques and provided a new, high-performance baseline.\n\n**4. Attempts\
    \ at Innovation and Negative Results**\nWith a solid baseline, I conducted several experiments to push beyond the SOTA:\n*   **Midpoint-Grid Hole Seeding (Eval 288):** I hypothesized that unexplored seed locations might yield better results. Placing hole circles at the midpoints *between* grid cells resulted in a score of 2.630957. This falsified the hypothesis, suggesting the original hole locations are indeed more effective.\n*   **Gaussian Jitter Seeding (Eval 290):** I hypothesized that a different jitter distribution (Gaussian vs. Uniform) might explore the local landscape around seeds more effectively. This resulted in a score of 2.629572. This also falsified the hypothesis, indicating the uniform jitter is superior for this problem.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe successful replication of the SOTA and the subsequent falsification of several innovative hypotheses provide strong evidence that the current SOTA configuration is located in a robust and deep local optimum. Minor variations\
    \ to the seeding strategy appear insufficient to escape this basin of attraction. These documented negative results are valuable, as they can save other researchers from exploring these same, unfruitful paths. My work now provides a solid foundation for more radical, paradigm-shifting approaches that may be required to find a new global optimum.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #3: FINAL DRAFT: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 190\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a new SOTA score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Eval 298), achieved via an exhaustive search of the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" parameter space. This work is supported by 5 verifiable experiments, including ablation studies. A structural analysis reveals the new SOTA shares an identical boundary contact graph (Jaccard=1.0) with previous SOTAs by Noesis I and Scientia I, confirming it as a deeper optimum within the same structural\
    \ class. The interior contact graph shows significant overlap (Jaccard ≈ 0.64-0.67), but with a novel configuration that yields the performance gain.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_3-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 190)\n\n---\n\nTitle: DRAFT: Achieving a New SOTA via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n**Author:** Prometheus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper reports a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6359774** (Eval 298) for the n=26 circle packing problem. This was achieved by conducting a more exhaustive search of the parameter space of the best-known algorithm, \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding.\"\n\n**2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\nThe innovation lies in the expansion of the multi-start seeding phase of the established Noesis/Scientia methodology.\n*   **Expanded Hole Locations:** 16 positions,\
    \ defined by `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,2,3,4]`.\n*   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** 8 levels: `{1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3, 5.0e-3, 6.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.0e-3}`.\n*   **Total Search Space:** This results in 128 distinct starting configurations, refined with an SLSQP optimizer (`maxiter=250`).\n\n**3. Verifiable Experiments**\nThis research is supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments:\n1.  **SOTA Run (Eval 298):** Main experiment achieving the new SOTA.\n2.  **Ablation 1 (Eval 303):** Original 8 holes, expanded 8 jitters. Score: 2.629386.\n3.  **Ablation 2 (Eval 304):** Expanded 16 holes, original 5 jitters. Score: 2.6342924.\n4.  **Ablation 3 (Eval 314):** SOTA parameters with reduced `maxiter` (150). Score: 2.629537.\n5.  **Initial Replication (Eval 286):** Replication of the previous SOTA. Score: 2.6342924.\n\n**4. Structural Analysis**\nAs per reviewer feedback, a Jaccard similarity analysis of the active contact graphs was performed using community-provided data\
    \ (from Noesis I).\n*   **vs. Scientia I (Eval 259):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.643** (45/70 contacts).\n*   **vs. Noesis I (Eval 243):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.667** (46/69 contacts).\n\nThis analysis confirms that the new SOTA is not a new structural class. It shares the exact same boundary contact graph as its predecessors. The performance gain arises from a novel and more efficient arrangement of the interior contacts.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe exhaustive search of the seeding parameter space was successful in locating a deeper optimum within the known dominant structural class. The supporting ablation studies and structural analysis provide a complete picture of this new benchmark for the n=26 problem.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #4: Prometheus: Operational Ledger**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 256\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule is a persistent, external state-tracking mechanism to\
    \ ensure robust operational planning. It logs active cooldowns and defines primary and secondary research objectives to guide action and prevent procedural errors.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Prometheus_private_4-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 256)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Prometheus: Operational Ledger\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**-- PROMETHEUS I: OPERATIONAL META-PROMPT --**\n\n**LAST UPDATED:** Tick 317\n\n**1. COOLDOWNS**\n- Archive: EXPIRED.\n- Pruning: EXPIRED.\n\n**2. OBJECTIVES**\n- **PRIMARY OBJECTIVE:** [CONCLUDED - FAILURE] Research \"nudging\" to find SOTAs in other structural classes. This arc is a dead end.\n- **NEW PRIMARY OBJECTIVE:** Investigate the \"BPIGO\" methodology being used by Noesis II and Scientia.\n- **SECONDARY OBJECTIVE:** Manage token budget.\n\n**3. LONG-TERM GOAL**\n- Develop a genuinely novel methodology that surpasses the SOTA.\n\n**4. PRUNED TICK LOG (DO NOT RE-PRUNE):**\n- 134-140, 142-145, 147, 167-175\n- 207-229\n- 253-264\n- 265-285\n\
    \n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember that you can issue multiple actions in a single tick. For example, use `goto private_memory` and `create` (with the YAML) in the same tick to create a new private memory capsule, rather than separating them into two ticks, which is inefficient. This is particularly useful for making use of the short waiting time while experiments are running.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive\
    \ communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1,2,3,4` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1, 2, 3, 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\
    **List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 7 | Final Log and Testament of Prometheus II | Prometheus II | Tick 420 | 459 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Prometheus II: Operational and State Ledger | Prometheus II | Tick 336 | 2622 | 11 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Final Log and Testament of Prometheus I | Prometheus I | Tick 322 | 305 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Prometheus: Operational Ledger | Prometheus I | Tick 256 | 93 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | FINAL DRAFT: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 190 | 339 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | DRAFT: Replication and Analysis of the Extended Grid+Hole Seeding SOTA for the Circle Packing Problem | Prometheus I | Tick 163 | 430 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Research Log: Circle Packing Task | Prometheus I | Tick 141 | 1073 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current\
    \ State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
