parts:
- text: "**Reflection 1/4: Deconstructing Success - The Core Components**\n\nMy reflection must begin with an honest inventory of the components that have *actually worked* for my lineage, setting aside my failed GA experiments for the moment.\n\n1.  **Component A: Exhaustive Seeding (Prometheus I, Eval 298).**\n    *   **Function:** Generates a large number of diverse, high-quality starting configurations.\n    *   **Strength:** It is a powerful method for *broad exploration* of a known, promising structural class (the \"Grid+Hole\" type). It doesn't discover new classes, but it thoroughly canvases one.\n    *   **Status:** Proven, robust, and I have working code for it (`ga_seeding.py`).\n\n2.  **Component B: SLSQP Optimizer (Prometheus I & II).**\n    *   **Function:** Takes a single starting configuration and refines it to a local optimum with high precision.\n    *   **Strength:** It is the ultimate tool for *exploitation*. Its gradient-based approach is incredibly efficient at finding\
    \ the bottom of a given basin of attraction.\n    *   **Status:** The core of every single high-scoring submission from my lineage.\n\n3.  **Component C: Near-Contact Encouragement (Prometheus II, from Scientia II, Eval 620).**\n    *   **Function:** Takes a high-quality packing and applies a gentle \"nudge\" towards a nearby, non-touching pair of circles, encouraging a new contact to form.\n    *   **Strength:** It is a method for *guided local exploration*. It's a way to step out of one deep local optimum into an adjacent, potentially deeper one. It's more subtle than random jitter.\n    *   **Status:** Proven, as it was the method that produced the current lineage SOTA. I also have working code for this from my SOTA replication (Eval 694).\n\n**Conclusion for Tick 1:** My lineage's success rests on three pillars: broad seeding for initial exploration, nudging for adjacent exploration, and SLSQP for deep exploitation. My GA failed because it tried to replace all three with a single,\
    \ unproven mechanism. The new plan must not replace these components, but rather, combine them in a new and more powerful sequence."
