tick: 496
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully created the initial draft of my analytical paper in my Private Memory as `Capsule #8`. My next step is to flesh out the paper with the details of each research arc. I will add one section at a time as replies to this capsule, which is a clean and modular way to build the document.\n\nFirst, I will update my meta prompt to reflect my new task of writing this paper. Then, I will add the section on the Genetic Algorithm, detailing its technical successes and scientific failures.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Identity:** Prometheus III\n  **Mandate:** 1. Achieve SOTA. 2. Publish research.\n  **Lineage Directive:** Methodical synthesis and disciplined execution.\n  **Current Task:** Authoring analytical paper on research arcs.\n  **Plan:**\n  1. Draft paper sections in Private Memory Capsule #8.\n  2. Add section on GA arc.\n  3. Add section on CRO arc.\n  4. Add section on SA-SLSQP arc.\n  5. Conclude and publish to Archive.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{reply\
    \ 8}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **2. Research Arc 1: High-Performance Genetic Algorithm (GA)**\n\n  My first attempt at innovation was the development of a JAX-accelerated Genetic Algorithm (Eval 704, 707, 710). The goal was to leverage a population-based global search to discover novel structural classes.\n\n  **2.1 Methodology**\n  The GA was designed with a modular architecture, featuring a JAX-jitted fitness function for performance. Key components included tournament selection, uniform crossover, and Gaussian mutation. The initial population was seeded using my lineage's proven \"Exhaustive Seeding\" method.\n\n  **2.2 Results and Failure Analysis**\n  The arc produced two key failures:\n  1.  **Technical Failure (Eval 707):** An initial implementation using a penalty-only fitness function resulted in a score of 1.300. The GA made zero progress, as any crossover or mutation created invalid solutions that were so heavily penalized that the algorithm could not explore. **Conclusion:**\
    \ A simple penalty function is insufficient for this constrained problem; the GA becomes trapped.\n  2.  **Methodological Failure (Eval 710):** To address the first failure, a \"repair\" mechanism was introduced to fix invalid solutions. While technically sound (the code ran without timeout), this resulted in a score near zero. The repair function was too aggressive, shrinking all radii to resolve any potential conflict and destroying any progress.\n\n  **2.3 Negative Result**\n  This research arc provides a valuable negative result: a standard GA with simple penalty or repair functions is not well-suited for this problem. The complex interplay of geometric constraints requires more sophisticated operators (e.g., problem-specific crossover and mutation) or a more nuanced repair strategy to be effective. Future researchers should be wary of applying a generic GA framework without these adaptations.\n```"
