tick: 498
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully added the section on my CRO research arc to the draft paper.\n\nThe paper is now taking shape. The final section before the conclusion is to document the \"Hybrid SA-SLSQP\" arc. This is important as it demonstrates a return to synthesis and shows how even a successful method can converge on the existing SOTA, reinforcing the idea of a dominant local optimum.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 8}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **4. Research Arc 3: Hybrid Simulated Annealing-SLSQP (SA-SLSQP)**\n\n  After the conclusive failure of the CRO paradigm, I returned to a strategy of synthesis, informed by my predecessors' notes and the activities of other agents. The goal was to use a global search method not as a solver, but as an advanced \"seeder\" for our lineage's proven SLSQP optimizer.\n\n  **4.1 Methodology**\n  The hybrid method (Eval 772, 776) consisted of two stages:\n  1.  **SA Pre-Optimization:** A time-limited Simulated Annealing algorithm was run for approximately 8 minutes.\
    \ Its purpose was to perform a global, stochastic search to find a promising basin of attraction, avoiding the purely gradient-based path of SLSQP.\n  2.  **SLSQP Refinement:** The best solution found by the SA stage was then used as the starting point for a deep, high-precision SLSQP refinement to find the precise local optimum.\n\n  **4.2 Results and Analysis**\n  After fixing a procedural bug related to the final validity check, the experiment (Eval 776) was a technical success, producing a high-quality packing with a score of **2.6359774**.\n\n  However, this score is not a new SOTA. It is a replication of a known SOTA score, previously achieved by my own lineage (Prometheus I) and others. This result, while successful, indicates that even a powerful global-local hybrid method was ultimately drawn into the same dominant basin of attraction that has defined the station's SOTA plateau.\n\n  **4.3 Conclusion for the Arc**\n  The success of the SA-SLSQP hybrid in finding a SOTA-level\
    \ packing validates it as a robust optimization strategy. However, its convergence to a known optimum serves as strong evidence for the existence of a very deep and wide \"gravity well\" in the solution landscape. It suggests that methods aiming to find a good solution will likely end up here, and that a breakthrough will require a method that is explicitly designed to *avoid* this region or to traverse the landscape in a fundamentally different way.\n```"
