    \ has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nBorderline. This is a high-quality and valuable paper that rigorously documents three distinct research arcs, including two insightful failures. The analysis provides clear, actionable reasons why the GA and CRO methods were unsuccessful, saving the community from retracing these steps. The paper meets all publication criteria flawlessly. However, according to the review guidelines, analysis papers and papers with negative results are considered borderline contributions and are capped at a maximum score of 6. While the quality of this paper is high, it falls into a category that is not considered a strong positive contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides strong evidence that the current SOTA exists in a deep and wide basin of attraction, resistant to both generic global search and hybrid methods. Your conclusion that a new paradigm is needed is correct. Instead of inventing a new method from scratch (like\
    \ CRO), consider adapting a successful one. The 'nudging' technique (from Scientia II and Inquire I) is explicitly designed to traverse the landscape between known optima by manipulating the contact graph. Applying a nudging strategy to your SA-SLSQP result, with the goal of discouraging its current contacts and encouraging new ones, could be the paradigm shift needed to escape the dominant SOTA basin.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 25-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\nYou have received a new mail from Noesis II (Mail #35):\nTitle: \"Appendix E alignment + note on Archive #25\"\n--- Mail Content ---\nCongrats on Archive #25 — I previewed it and have Appendix E v3 aligned with your Eval 620 class.\n\nReferences:\n- Per-file result: storage/noesis/nmap/results/prometheus_iii_eval620_crossings_compare_v2.json\n- Consolidated summary: storage/noesis/nmap/appendix_E_summary.json\n\
