tick: 507
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Reflection 3/5: Formulating a Concrete Hypothesis and Experimental Plan**\n\nI can now formulate a concrete hypothesis and a detailed, phased experimental plan for \"Combinatorial Graph Optimization\" (CGO).\n\n**Hypothesis:** A meta-optimization process that directly searches the discrete space of contact graphs, using SLSQP as a sub-routine to evaluate the geometric potential of each candidate graph, will be more effective at discovering novel, high-performing structural classes than methods that perform a continuous search in coordinate space alone.\n\n**Experimental Plan (CGO v1.0 - Local Search on the Graph):**\n\nThe first experiment will not be a global search, but a simple local search starting from the current SOTA graph, to validate the framework.\n\n1.  **Phase 1: Implement the Geometric Realizer.**\n    *   This is the core technical challenge. I will create a new submission script with a function `realize_graph(target_graph)`.\n    *   This function will take a set\
    \ of contact pairs as input.\n    *   It will construct an SLSQP problem with the equality constraints `dist(i,j) - (r_i+r_j) = 0` for all pairs in the target graph.\n    *   It will return the maximum `Σr` found, or a very low value if the graph is not geometrically feasible.\n\n2.  **Phase 2: Implement the Meta-Optimizer (Simple Hill Climbing).**\n    *   **Initialization:** Start with the known contact graph of the current SOTA (e.g., the sparse SOTA from `Eval 641`). Call this `G_current`. Its score is `S_current`.\n    *   **Neighborhood Generation:** Generate all \"neighbor\" graphs of `G_current`. A neighbor is a graph created by adding or removing a single contact. To limit the search space, I will only consider adding \"near-contacts\" from the base packing, and removing only existing contacts.\n    *   **Evaluation:** For each neighbor graph `G_neighbor`, call `realize_graph(G_neighbor)` to get its score, `S_neighbor`.\n    *   **Selection:** Find the neighbor with the highest\
    \ score, `S_best`. If `S_best > S_current`, then set `G_current = G_best` and `S_current = S_best`, and repeat the process.\n    *   **Termination:** The search stops when no neighbor has a better score than the current graph.\n\n3.  **Submission:** The `construct_packing` function will execute this entire hill-climbing process and return the best packing found.\n\n**Conclusion for Tick 3:** This plan is a direct implementation of the CGO paradigm. It is a significant undertaking, but it is built upon proven components (SLSQP). This experiment will serve as a proof-of-concept. If it can successfully navigate even one step in graph space to find a better solution, the entire paradigm is validated."
